home_content_age
Go
-----
home_content_agf
525 MB
-----
home_content_agg
1m 33s
-----
home_content_agh
Rust
-----
home_content_agi
30 GB
-----
home_content_agj
45m
-----
home_content_aha
GCC
-----
home_content_ahb
8 GB
-----
home_content_ahc
50m
-----
home_content_ahd
Clang
-----
home_content_ahe
90 GB
-----
home_content_ahf
60m
-----
home_content_ahg
Swift
-----
home_content_ahh
70 GB
-----
home_content_ahi
90m
-----
home_content_ahj
V
-----
home_content_aia
< 20 MB
-----
home_content_aib
<1s
-----
home_content_aic
Flexible memory management
-----
home_content_aid
V avoids doing unnecessary allocations in the first place by using value types, string buffers, promoting a simple abstraction-free code style.
-----
home_content_aie
There are 4 ways to manage memory in V.
-----
home_content_aif
The default is a minimal and a well performing tracing GC.
-----
home_content_aig
The second way is autofree, it can be enabled with
-----
home_content_aih
. It takes care of most objects (~90-100%): the compiler inserts necessary free calls automatically during compilation. Remaining small percentage of objects is freed via GC. The developer doesn't need to change anything in their code. "It just works", like in Python, Go, or Java, except there's no heavy GC tracing everything or expensive RC for each object. Autofree is still experimental and not production ready yet. That's planned for V 1.0.
-----
home_content_aii
For developers willing to have more low level control, memory can be managed manually with
-----
home_content_aij
Arena allocation is available via
-----
home_content_aja
V's autofree demo. All objects are freed during compilation. Running the Ved editor on an 8 MB file with 0 leaks:
-----
home_content_ajb
Youtube Compile Speed
-----
home_content_ajc
C translation
-----
home_content_ajd
V can translate your entire C project and offer you the safety, simplicity, and compilation speed-up (via modules).
-----
home_content_aje
A
-----
home_content_ajf
blog post/tutorial
-----
home_content_ajg
about translating DOOM is available.
-----
home_content_ajh
C++ to V translation is at an early stage.
-----
home_content_aji
Hot code reloading
-----
home_content_ajj
Get your changes instantly without recompiling.
-----
home_content_baa
Since you also don't have to get to the state you are working on after every compilation, this can save a lot of precious minutes of your development time.
-----
home_content_bab
github.com/.../examples/hot_reload
-----
home_content_bac
A powerful graphics library
-----
home_content_bad
Cross-platform drawing library
-----
home_content_bae
, using OpenGL/Metal/DirectX 11 for rendering 2D applications.
-----
home_content_baf
There's also a 3D engine in development with the following features already available:
-----
home_content_bag
Loading complex 3D objects with textures
-----
home_content_bah
Camera (moving, looking around)
-----
home_content_bai
Skeletal animation
-----
home_content_baj
A simple example of the graphics library in action is
-----
home_content_bba
tetris.v
-----
home_content_bbb
For 3D examples, check out
-----
home_content_bbc
this
-----
home_content_bbd
Engine Screenshot
-----
home_content_bbe
Light and fast cross-platform GUI library
-----
home_content_bbf
Build native UI apps with V UI. You no longer need to embed a browser to develop cross-platform apps quickly.
-----
home_content_bbg
V has a UI module that uses custom drawing, similar to Qt and Flutter, but with as much similarity to the native GUI toolkit as possible.
-----
home_content_bbh
It has a declarative API similar to SwiftUI and React Native and runs on Windows, Linux, macOS, and Android.
-----
home_content_bbi
Coming soon:
-----
home_content_bbj
a Delphi-like visual editor for building native GUI apps
-----
home_content_bca
iOS support
-----
home_content_bcb
github.com/vlang/ui
-----
home_content_bcc
Volt, a 3 MB Slack/Discord client built with V and V UI:
-----
home_content_bcd
Screen3
-----
home_content_bce
Easy cross compilation
-----
home_content_bcf
To cross compile your software simply run
-----
home_content_bcg
or
-----
home_content_bch
. No extra steps required, even for GUI and graphical apps!
-----
home_content_bci
(Compiling macOS software only works on macOS for now.)
-----
home_content_bcj
Building V for Windows using V for macOS, and then testing resulting v.exe on a Windows VM:
-----
home_content_bda
Painless deployments and dependency management & Run everywhere
-----
home_content_bdb
Painless deployments and dependency management
-----
home_content_bdc
To build your project, no matter how big, all you need to do is run
-----
home_content_bdd
No build environments, makefiles, headers, virtual environments, etc.
-----
home_content_bde
You get a single binary that is guaranteed to work on all operating systems (provided you cross compile) without any external dependencies.
-----
home_content_bdf
This binary is self-contained except for the OS-provided system library (libc), which is linked dynamically.
-----
home_content_bdg
To achieve fully static binaries on Linux, you can use
-----
home_content_bdh
Installing new libraries via vpm, a centralized package manager written in V, is as simple as
-----
home_content_bdi
Run everywhere
-----
home_content_bdj
V can emit (human readable) C, so you get the great platform support and optimization of GCC and Clang. (Use
-----
home_content_bea
to make optimized builds.)
-----
home_content_beb
Emitting C will always be an option, even after direct machine code generation matures.
-----
home_content_bec
V can call C code, and calling V code is possible in any language that has C interop.
-----
home_content_bed
REPL & Cross-platform shell scripts in V
-----
home_content_bee
REPL
-----
home_content_bef
Cross-platform shell scripts in V
-----
home_content_beg
V can be used as an alternative to Bash to write deployment scripts, build scripts, etc. The advantage of using V for this is the simplicity and predictability of the language, and cross-platform support. "V scripts" run on Unix-like systems as well as on Windows.
-----
home_content_beh
Read more about V script
-----
home_content_bei
Code formatting with vfmt for consistent style & A built-in code profiler
-----
home_content_bej
Code formatting with vfmt for consistent style
-----
home_content_bfa
No more arguments about coding styles. There's one official coding style enforced by the vfmt formatter.
-----
home_content_bfb
All V code bases are guaranteed to use the same style, making it easier to read and change code written by other developers.
-----
home_content_bfc
A built-in code profiler
-----
home_content_bfd
Build and run your program with
-----
home_content_bfe
and you'll get a detailed list for all function calls: number of calls, average time per call, total time per call.
-----
home_content_bff
JavaScript and WASM backends & Automatic documentation
-----
home_content_bfg
JavaScript and WASM backends
-----
home_content_bfh
V programs can be translated to JavaScript (WIP):
-----
home_content_bfi
The JS backend is at an early stage.
-----
home_content_bfj
They can also be compiled to WASM (for now with Emscripten, but native WASM support is planned). V compiler compiled to WASM and running V programs by translating them to JavaScript:
-----
home_content_bga
v-wasm.vercel.app
-----
home_content_bgb
A game written using V's graphical backend and compiled to WASM:
-----
home_content_bgc
v2048
-----
home_content_bgd
Automatic documentation
-----
home_content_bge
Use vdoc to get instant documentation generated directly from the module's source code. No need to keep and update separate documentation.
-----
home_content_bgf
